# the x86 extension catalog, its levels and its selection
#
# a row is a feature a selection holds: an instruction set a CPUID bit enumerates, or an
# architectural state the psABI names. each row is spelled once, as mach's manifest already spells
# the ones it has (sse41, sse42, cx16, pclmul, fsgsbase) and as XED spells the rest, in lower case,
# because the name index holds one entry per row and a row cannot answer to two spellings. the
# spellings of the rest are the work of a consumer's syntax, not aliases here. an ISA set of XED that
# is no row of its own, such as avx512bw_128 or apx_f_adx, is a requirement over rows, which the XED
# reader states (#29)
#
# the code width is execution state, not a row: the axis width of 16, 32 and 64 bits, which code
# switches (`.code16`, `.code32`, `.code64`), so a register or instruction that exists at one width
# requires its state value, as rax requires width=64. doitm, the data operand independent timing
# mode of IA32_UARCH_MISC_CTL (SDM Volume 1, 18.1), is the other axis, which the operating system
# sets and code does not switch. a flag an operating system sets to enable a feature is part of the feature's row
# (pku and ospku, klsupported and klenabled, a padlock unit and its enable bit), except where a
# source names the state itself: osxsave, osfxsr and sce, which the psABI lists. the ISA sets of
# XED that no CPUID bit enumerates and every x86 processor executes (i86 to i486, pentiumreal, ppro,
# pause and the nops) have no row, and their requirement is none
#
# an implication is stated only where a source states it, and the source is named at the row. the
# sources, all read at the revisions named:
#   Intel SDM 325462-093US (September 2026), Volume 1
#     10.5.1.2 sse: SSE instructions cannot be used unless CR4.OSFXSR is 1
#     13.2   osxsave: CR4.OSXSAVE can be set if and only if CPUID.01H:ECX.XSAVE is 1
#     13.3   avx, avx-512, mpx and amx: their instructions execute only with CR4.OSXSAVE 1, and amx
#            only in 64-bit mode
#     16.2   avx10: versions are inclusive, and every processor with AVX10 also has AVX, AVX2 and
#            AVX-512. Table 16-2 lists the AVX-512 families AVX10.1 includes. vaes, vpclmulqdq and
#            gfni stay enumerated by their own bits, so their EVEX forms need both the feature and
#            the version (avx10_1 does not imply them)
#     21.3   CPUID leaves: the bits the XED table below names
#     5.22   vmfunc: the instruction is invoked in VMX non-root operation, so it needs vmx
#   Intel Instruction Set Extensions and Future Features 319433-062 (June 2026)
#     1.4    Table 1-3: CPUID leaves 07H.01H, 1EH and 24H
#   Intel AVX10.2 Architecture Specification 361050-005US (June 2025)
#     3.1.2  avx10_vnni_int: a processor with AVX10_VNNI_INT also enumerates AVX10.2. AMX-AVX512 is
#            no longer sensitive to AVX10.2 (revision 4.0), so amx_avx512 implies amx_tile only and
#            an instruction of it also requires avx512f or an avx10 version
#   Intel APX Architecture Specification 355828-009US (August 2026)
#     3.1    apx_f is available only in 64-bit mode (3.1.4.2.1) and only with CR4.OSXSAVE 1 (Table
#            3.8). every processor with apx_f also enumerates apx_nci_ndd_nf, and one with both also
#            enumerates cmov (3.1.4.3.1), which is the catalog's one combination
#   x86-64 psABI e1ce098331da5dbd66e1ffc74162380bcc213236 (2025-03-12), low-level-sys-info.tex
#     Table "Micro-Architecture Levels": the baseline and the levels x86-64-v2 to x86-64-v4, which
#            are cumulative. the baseline has no level of its own in the psABI and none here
#   AMD64 Architecture Programmer's Manual Volume 3, 24594 revision 3.28 (September 2019)
#     D.2    Table D-1: the AMD instruction subsets and their CPUID flags
#   XED 0bcb6237345c5066726dcc08b3d87928df3b5b26 (2026-08-24), datafiles/**/cpuid*.txt
#     the CPUID names of every ISA set and which sets need more than one (fma needs avx, vaes needs
#            aes, sha512 needs avx)
#   LLVM bd58c858ea9b5a41716cd8e09935904cf2470d7b, llvm/lib/TargetParser/X86TargetParser.cpp
#     the ImpliedFeatures chains where no Intel or AMD document states one: sse to sse4.2, avx and
#            avx2, avx-512 on avx512f and avx512bw, and the vector extensions on their widths
#
# the closures agree with LLVM's wherever LLVM has the feature, and with the macros clang defines for
# each feature it is given (__AVX2__ and the rest), except in five places, each for the source
# named. sha512 and sm4 imply avx, where LLVM implies avx2 (XED gives sha512 and avx, sm4 and avx).
# the amx and apx rows imply osxsave, which LLVM's table does not model, and that they exist only in
# 64-bit mode is their instructions' requirement of width=64, as no row implies a state value. avx implies
# osxsave and so xsave, which clang's driver also enables with avx. amx_avx512 implies amx_tile,
# where LLVM implies avx10_2 (the specification removed that sensitivity). apx_f is one row, where
# LLVM splits it into nine features that the CPUID leaves do not enumerate singly
#
# the levels hold extensions only, though the psABI defines them for 64-bit code: the width an
# object enters is its architecture's, and x86-64-v3 lists osxsave as the psABI does, with the xsave
# it brings

use std.types.bool.bool;
use std.types.bool.false;
use std.types.bool.true;
use std.types.result.res;
use std.types.size.usize;
use std.types.string.str;
use std.types.string.str_compare;
use std.types.string.str_equals;

use alloc: std.allocator;
use std.allocator.testing;

use masc.core.diagnostic;
use masc.core.extension;
use masc.core.index;
use masc.core.requirement;
use masc.core.set;
use masc.core.state;

use mink;

# the ids: each extension's row, in catalog order. every id follows the one before it, so a row inserted keeps the order

# baseline and the sse chain
pub val CX8:    requirement.Id = 0;
pub val CMOV:   requirement.Id = CX8 + 1;
pub val X87:    requirement.Id = CMOV + 1;
pub val FXSR:   requirement.Id = X87 + 1;
pub val OSFXSR: requirement.Id = FXSR + 1;
pub val MMX:    requirement.Id = OSFXSR + 1;
pub val SCE:    requirement.Id = MMX + 1;
pub val SSE:    requirement.Id = SCE + 1;
pub val SSE2:   requirement.Id = SSE + 1;
pub val SSE3:   requirement.Id = SSE2 + 1;
pub val SSSE3:  requirement.Id = SSE3 + 1;
pub val SSE41:  requirement.Id = SSSE3 + 1;
pub val SSE42:  requirement.Id = SSE41 + 1;
pub val CX16:   requirement.Id = SSE42 + 1;
pub val LAHF:   requirement.Id = CX16 + 1;
pub val POPCNT: requirement.Id = LAHF + 1;
pub val MOVBE:  requirement.Id = POPCNT + 1;

# system and state management
pub val SEP:            requirement.Id = MOVBE + 1;
pub val RDTSCP:         requirement.Id = SEP + 1;
pub val CLFLUSH:        requirement.Id = RDTSCP + 1;
pub val PREFETCHW:      requirement.Id = CLFLUSH + 1;
pub val PREFETCHWT1:    requirement.Id = PREFETCHW + 1;
pub val MONITOR:        requirement.Id = PREFETCHWT1 + 1;
pub val SMX:            requirement.Id = MONITOR + 1;
pub val VMX:            requirement.Id = SMX + 1;
pub val XSAVE:          requirement.Id = VMX + 1;
pub val OSXSAVE:        requirement.Id = XSAVE + 1;
pub val XSAVEOPT:       requirement.Id = OSXSAVE + 1;
pub val XSAVEC:         requirement.Id = XSAVEOPT + 1;
pub val XSAVES:         requirement.Id = XSAVEC + 1;
pub val FSGSBASE:       requirement.Id = XSAVES + 1;
pub val INVPCID:        requirement.Id = FSGSBASE + 1;
pub val SMAP:           requirement.Id = INVPCID + 1;
pub val PKU:            requirement.Id = SMAP + 1;
pub val RDPID:          requirement.Id = PKU + 1;
pub val SGX:            requirement.Id = RDPID + 1;
pub val CET:            requirement.Id = SGX + 1;
pub val HRESET:         requirement.Id = CET + 1;
pub val LKGS:           requirement.Id = HRESET + 1;
pub val FRED:           requirement.Id = LKGS + 1;
pub val WRMSRNS:        requirement.Id = FRED + 1;
pub val MSRLIST:        requirement.Id = WRMSRNS + 1;
pub val MSR_IMM:        requirement.Id = MSRLIST + 1;
pub val USER_MSR:       requirement.Id = MSR_IMM + 1;
pub val UINTR:          requirement.Id = USER_MSR + 1;
pub val SERIALIZE:      requirement.Id = UINTR + 1;
pub val WAITPKG:        requirement.Id = SERIALIZE + 1;
pub val WBNOINVD:       requirement.Id = WAITPKG + 1;
pub val PCONFIG:        requirement.Id = WBNOINVD + 1;
pub val PBNDKB:         requirement.Id = PCONFIG + 1;
pub val IBHF:           requirement.Id = PBNDKB + 1;
pub val PTWRITE:        requirement.Id = IBHF + 1;
pub val KEYLOCKER:      requirement.Id = PTWRITE + 1;
pub val KEYLOCKER_WIDE: requirement.Id = KEYLOCKER + 1;
pub val MPX:            requirement.Id = KEYLOCKER_WIDE + 1;
pub val RTM:            requirement.Id = MPX + 1;
pub val TSX_LDTRK:      requirement.Id = RTM + 1;
pub val TDX:            requirement.Id = TSX_LDTRK + 1;
pub val VMFUNC:         requirement.Id = TDX + 1;

# memory and ordering instructions
pub val CLFLUSHOPT:      requirement.Id = VMFUNC + 1;
pub val CLWB:            requirement.Id = CLFLUSHOPT + 1;
pub val CLDEMOTE:        requirement.Id = CLWB + 1;
pub val MOVDIRI:         requirement.Id = CLDEMOTE + 1;
pub val MOVDIR64B:       requirement.Id = MOVDIRI + 1;
pub val ENQCMD:          requirement.Id = MOVDIR64B + 1;
pub val RAO_INT:         requirement.Id = ENQCMD + 1;
pub val CMPCCXADD:       requirement.Id = RAO_INT + 1;
pub val MOVRS:           requirement.Id = CMPCCXADD + 1;
pub val ICACHE_PREFETCH: requirement.Id = MOVRS + 1;
pub val RDRAND:          requirement.Id = ICACHE_PREFETCH + 1;
pub val RDSEED:          requirement.Id = RDRAND + 1;
pub val ADX:             requirement.Id = RDSEED + 1;

# bit manipulation
pub val BMI1:  requirement.Id = ADX + 1;
pub val BMI2:  requirement.Id = BMI1 + 1;
pub val LZCNT: requirement.Id = BMI2 + 1;

# crypto and carry-less
pub val AES:    requirement.Id = LZCNT + 1;
pub val PCLMUL: requirement.Id = AES + 1;
pub val SHA:    requirement.Id = PCLMUL + 1;
pub val GFNI:   requirement.Id = SHA + 1;

# avx
pub val AVX:            requirement.Id = GFNI + 1;
